> ## Key issues
> 
> 1. **Partial PWT coverage creates historical holes.**  
>    `no_pwt` is defined at country level, so a country with *any* calibrated PWT year will retain `NA` for its PWT-missing years:
>    ```r
>    no_pwt <- setdiff(all_iso, has_iso)
>    ```
>    If the promised output is a complete 1990–2100 panel, fallback should be applied per `iso3 × year`, not only for countries with no PWT ever.
> 
> 2. **Venezuela replacement can delete IRs.**  
>    This removes all Venezuelan rows from 2012 onward:
>    ```r
>    baseline <- baseline[!(iso3 == "VEN" & year >= 2012)]
>    ```
>    But `venezuela_income()` only returns IR-years with valid Kummu IR and national values. Previously populated fallback IRs, or IRs missing raster values, disappear entirely. Replace only observations actually supplied by `ven`, or construct a complete Venezuelan replacement panel.
> 
> 3. **`interp_annual()` silently drops countries with one valid knot.**  
>    For `.N < 2`, it returns no rows. This can unexpectedly eliminate countries from SSP coverage. Consider retaining the sole year or explicitly erroring/flagging it.
> 
> 4. **Input/model validation is missing.**  
>    Any `gdp_model` other than `"IIASA"` falls through to the OECD branch:
>    ```r
>    if (gdp_model == "IIASA") ... else ...
>    ```
>    Add:
>    ```r
>    stopifnot(gdp_model %chin% c("OECD", "IIASA"))
>    ```
>    and validate `scen`, required columns, unique keys, and positive GDP values.
> 
> ## Robustness / maintainability
> 
> 5. **`dcast()` plus `setnames()` assumes both years exist.**  
>    If either 2022 or 2023 is absent globally, this errors:
>    ```r
>    setnames(pw, c("2022", "2023"), c("p22", "p23"))
>    ```
>    Ensure missing columns are created as `NA_real_` before renaming.
> 
> 6. **Global geometric means need finite-value guards.**  
>    Both:
>    ```r
>    exp(mean(log(os$g_ssp)))
>    exp(mean(log(growth)))
>    ```
>    yield `NaN` for empty inputs and propagate non-finite values. Filter with `is.finite(growth) & growth > 0`, then fail clearly if no fallback can be calculated.
> 
> 7. **Assert uniqueness before merges.**  
>    The code assumes unique `iso3, year` national series and unique `hierid, year` IR series. Add assertions before joins to prevent duplicate-driven multiplication or cartesian-join failures.
> 
> 8. **The national-rescaling logic deserves an invariant test.**  
>    After `force_income_to_national()`, verify for every covered country-year:
>    ```r
>    sum(gdppc * pop) / sum(pop) == nat_gdppc
>    ```
>    within a numerical tolerance. This is the central accounting guarantee of that delta.
> 
> Overall, the construction is coherent and well-commented; I would fix the partial-PWT and Venezuela row-loss issues before relying on the panel.
